  "CustomerSummary": "This case involves approximately 14,000 retail customers for whom CUNA Brokerage Services made recommendations regarding rollovers of employer-sponsored retirement plans into individual retirement accounts. The firm failed to preserve records containing information collected from these customers pursuant to Regulation Best Interest. The records included important customer information such as whether a potential transfer was a forced distribution, the importance to customers of protecting retirement assets from creditors or legal judgments, and the importance of flexibility in distribution options. The breach was a recordkeeping violation rather than a Care Obligation violation.",
  "OverallSummary": "This case involves CUNA Brokerage Services' failure to preserve approximately 14,000 records containing information collected from retail customers between June 30, 2020, and January 31, 2022, when making recommendations regarding rollovers from employer-sponsored retirement plans to IRAs. The records were inadvertently deleted in January 2023 during a business transition. These records contained information collected pursuant to Regulation Best Interest, including details about forced distributions, the importance of creditor protection, and the importance of distribution flexibility to customers. This case does not involve a Care Obligation violation but rather a recordkeeping violation of Section 17(a) of the Exchange Act, Exchange Act Rule 17a-4(e)(5), and FINRA Rules 4511 and 2010. No financial losses to customers were specified in the case. The firm was censured and fined $30,000 for these violations."
